+## Staging deployment
+
+The staging definition binds the editor only to loopback. Caddy is the public
+HTTPS and WebSocket entry point, so the container is never exposed directly to
+the internet.
+
+On the server, copy and edit the environment template without committing the
+result:
+
+```powershell
+Copy-Item .\docker\ralue\staging.env.example .\docker\ralue\staging.env
+docker compose --env-file .\docker\ralue\staging.env `
+  -f .\docker\ralue\compose.staging.yml config --quiet
+docker compose --env-file .\docker\ralue\staging.env `
+  -f .\docker\ralue\compose.staging.yml up -d
+```
+
+Copy `Caddyfile.example` to the server's active Caddy configuration and load
+the same `RALUE_EDITOR_DOMAIN` and `RALUE_EDITOR_PORT` values into Caddy's
+service environment. Validate before reloading:
+
+```powershell
+caddy validate --config .\docker\ralue\Caddyfile
+caddy reload --config .\docker\ralue\Caddyfile
+```
+
+The definition includes a health check, automatic restart, bounded Docker log
+rotation, and configurable CPU, RAM, process, and shared-memory limits. The
+defaults are a staging starting point, not a capacity guarantee. Keep WOPI
+storage and database backups outside this stateless editor container.
+
+After deployment, verify all three routes through the public hostname:
+
+```text
+/hosting/discovery
+/hosting/capabilities
+/browser/a1e2604001/cool.html
+```
+
+Then run an authenticated open, edit, save, close, and reopen test through the
+real Ralue application before promoting the image to production.
+
